1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
Simply boil water to see what it can do.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:08,000
Provide locomotion, generate electricity,

3
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:13,000
clean just about anything, and power outlandish gizmos.

4
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:18,000
But if you don't watch out, it can huff and puff

5
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:20,000
and blow your house down.

6
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:23,000
All right, ready to go balls out, ready to go full steam ahead?

7
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:25,000
You'll find out where all these terms come from.

8
00:00:25,001 --> 00:00:26,000
Coming up.

9
00:00:26,001 --> 00:00:32,580
Now, Steam on Modern Marvels.

10
00:00:44,720 --> 00:00:45,660
Steam cars.

11
00:00:46,600 --> 00:00:48,360
In the early days of the automobile,

12
00:00:48,940 --> 00:00:51,360
they were more popular than the internal combustion engine.

13
00:00:53,400 --> 00:00:55,060
To learn about them from an expert,

14
00:00:55,061 --> 00:00:57,460
we went to Jay Leno's garage.

15
00:00:57,560 --> 00:01:01,040
In two enormous buildings, Jay has amassed a collection

16
00:01:01,140 --> 00:01:03,860
of exceptional and unusual motor vehicles,

17
00:01:03,960 --> 00:01:05,640
including some classic steamers.

18
00:01:05,740 --> 00:01:08,800
What we're going to do right now is fire up a 1909

19
00:01:08,900 --> 00:01:11,140
Stanley steam car.

20
00:01:11,240 --> 00:01:13,780
Steam cars were quite popular at the turn of the century

21
00:01:13,880 --> 00:01:15,880
because, don't forget, cars back in the early days,

22
00:01:15,980 --> 00:01:17,820
you had to crank them, and you had a magneto,

23
00:01:17,920 --> 00:01:19,020
and you had to set the choke.

24
00:01:19,120 --> 00:01:20,220
And a lot of times, they'd backfire,

25
00:01:20,320 --> 00:01:22,080
and people would break their arms.

26
00:01:22,081 --> 00:01:25,780
Where steam, well, steam technology was 100 years old

27
00:01:25,880 --> 00:01:27,320
by the time this car was built.

28
00:01:27,420 --> 00:01:29,520
Steam technology was easily understood.

29
00:01:29,620 --> 00:01:31,620
You heat up water, it makes steam,

30
00:01:31,720 --> 00:01:34,720
it pushes the piston, and you go.

31
00:01:34,820 --> 00:01:35,820
Let's open up the hood.

32
00:01:39,240 --> 00:01:43,240
OK, not as pretty as looking at a 289 Mustang or 327 Chevy,

33
00:01:43,340 --> 00:01:45,040
but this is not your engine.

34
00:01:45,140 --> 00:01:47,440
This is just your boiler.

35
00:01:47,540 --> 00:01:50,480
Getting an old steamer ready to run isn't simple.

36
00:01:50,481 --> 00:01:53,780
It's a multi-step, time-consuming process.

37
00:01:53,880 --> 00:01:56,620
And firing up the boiler can be an adventure.

38
00:01:56,720 --> 00:01:59,020
This is one of the few cars you can get burned to death

39
00:01:59,120 --> 00:02:01,920
and scalded to death at the same time.

40
00:02:02,020 --> 00:02:04,020
I mean, you're carrying an open flame,

41
00:02:04,120 --> 00:02:07,240
and you're made of wood.

42
00:02:07,340 --> 00:02:08,940
Adding air pressure?

43
00:02:09,040 --> 00:02:10,440
Easy.

44
00:02:10,540 --> 00:02:11,340
Pilot light?

45
00:02:11,440 --> 00:02:12,440
No problem.

46
00:02:12,540 --> 00:02:15,180
But then Jay's first attempt to ignite the gas injectors

47
00:02:15,280 --> 00:02:18,080
that provide the heat for the boiler fails.

48
00:02:18,180 --> 00:02:19,740
Not good.

49
00:02:19,741 --> 00:02:20,980
OK, that's a bad sign.

50
00:02:27,980 --> 00:02:30,280
Now he's not sure how much vaporized gas

51
00:02:30,380 --> 00:02:31,980
is floating around.

52
00:02:32,080 --> 00:02:33,880
Let's take a chance.

53
00:02:33,980 --> 00:02:36,500
Here you go.

54
00:02:36,600 --> 00:02:38,800
Exactly what I'm talking about.

55
00:02:38,900 --> 00:02:42,200
As you can see, it has burned.

56
00:02:42,300 --> 00:02:44,840
See, when you go home, your wife knows where you've been.

57
00:02:44,940 --> 00:02:47,540
She can't accuse you of being out or doing something

58
00:02:47,541 --> 00:02:50,200
when your arm is on fire.

59
00:02:50,300 --> 00:02:52,640
Jay has better luck the second time around,

60
00:02:52,740 --> 00:02:54,240
and soon the boiler is warming up.

61
00:02:57,180 --> 00:02:59,880
The car lets out a loud moan as the metal expands.

62
00:03:03,180 --> 00:03:06,800
Next, Jay pushes the car outside to fill up the car's 30-gallon

63
00:03:06,900 --> 00:03:08,900
water tank.

64
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:10,400
I'm slowly building steam pressure.

65
00:03:10,500 --> 00:03:11,300
Let's see if we got any.

66
00:03:11,400 --> 00:03:12,100
I'll check my whistle.

67
00:03:14,840 --> 00:03:15,900
I'm getting there.

68
00:03:15,901 --> 00:03:19,100
See, the nice thing about steam is a gasoline engine

69
00:03:19,200 --> 00:03:21,600
might turn 3,500 revolutions per minute.

70
00:03:21,700 --> 00:03:25,400
This engine turns about 357 revolutions per mile.

71
00:03:25,500 --> 00:03:28,340
It's just like that.

72
00:03:28,440 --> 00:03:30,340
So when you pull away, dead silent.

73
00:03:37,560 --> 00:03:39,060
There's no gears to shift.

74
00:03:39,160 --> 00:03:40,060
There's no transmission.

75
00:03:40,160 --> 00:03:41,200
There's no clutch.

76
00:03:41,300 --> 00:03:45,200
Steam is so powerful, you don't need gears.

77
00:03:45,201 --> 00:03:48,460
A steam engine is known as an external combustion engine

78
00:03:48,560 --> 00:03:51,340
because unlike an internal combustion engine,

79
00:03:51,440 --> 00:03:53,840
the power is produced outside of the engine,

80
00:03:53,940 --> 00:03:57,440
in a boiler or a steam generator.

81
00:03:57,540 --> 00:04:01,040
When water boils, it converts from a liquid to a gas.

82
00:04:01,140 --> 00:04:03,360
It expands in the process, creating

83
00:04:03,460 --> 00:04:05,660
pressure in a closed system.

84
00:04:05,760 --> 00:04:07,360
That pressure can be used to do work,

85
00:04:07,460 --> 00:04:10,300
like running pistons in Jay's Stanley steamer.

86
00:04:10,400 --> 00:04:14,100
While inside a pressurized system, steam is invisible.

87
00:04:14,101 --> 00:04:16,100
It's when it vents into cooler temperatures

88
00:04:16,200 --> 00:04:20,800
that it condenses into white clouds of water vapor.

89
00:04:20,900 --> 00:04:22,900
This is your throttle right here.

90
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:26,840
And you open your throttle, and you go.

91
00:04:26,940 --> 00:04:28,740
Let's see if we can beat the camera car.

92
00:04:36,160 --> 00:04:39,300
Jay has hit 70 miles per hour in this car.

93
00:04:39,400 --> 00:04:41,360
Not bad.

94
00:04:41,460 --> 00:04:43,500
But the Stanley steamer land speed record

95
00:04:43,501 --> 00:04:48,800
is 127 miles per hour, set in 1906.

96
00:04:48,900 --> 00:04:50,500
When you drive a steam car, you learn

97
00:04:50,600 --> 00:04:53,680
to shut off your throttle as you approach the stop sign,

98
00:04:53,780 --> 00:04:55,320
so you get a buildup of steam.

99
00:04:55,420 --> 00:04:57,020
You don't have limitless steam.

100
00:04:57,120 --> 00:04:59,980
You're actually using steam faster than you're making it.

101
00:05:00,080 --> 00:05:03,020
So you want to try to conserve as much steam as you can.

102
00:05:07,120 --> 00:05:10,300
After taking a spin in his Stanley,

103
00:05:10,400 --> 00:05:13,500
Jay decides to roll out one of his more elegant steamers.

104
00:05:13,501 --> 00:05:16,900
a 1907 white steam car.

105
00:05:16,901 --> 00:05:19,140
This was a bit more expensive,

106
00:05:19,141 --> 00:05:21,500
a bit more prestigious than the Stanley steam car.

107
00:05:21,501 --> 00:05:23,140
This was a step up.

108
00:05:23,141 --> 00:05:25,140
They built 10,000 of these.

109
00:05:25,141 --> 00:05:26,940
This was the first White House car.

110
00:05:26,941 --> 00:05:30,580
Teddy Roosevelt rode in a white steamer.

111
00:05:30,581 --> 00:05:32,980
Starting up this car doesn't take as long

112
00:05:32,981 --> 00:05:36,020
because of its more efficient way of producing steam.

113
00:05:36,021 --> 00:05:39,020
With the Stanley, you're heating up 15 gallons of water.

114
00:05:39,021 --> 00:05:41,620
You have a big fire, like a giant kettle.

115
00:05:41,621 --> 00:05:44,620
With this, you're never heating more than two quarts of water at a time.

116
00:05:48,620 --> 00:05:52,620
As you can see, the white was a lot more relaxed version of the Stanley.

117
00:05:52,621 --> 00:05:54,620
Not quite as hectic.

118
00:05:54,621 --> 00:05:56,620
Big, comfortable cars.

119
00:05:56,621 --> 00:06:00,620
35 miles an hour is a nice cruising speed.

120
00:06:00,621 --> 00:06:02,620
This is what they call a condensing car.

121
00:06:02,621 --> 00:06:06,620
If you notice, unlike the Stanley, you don't see plumes of steam coming out the back

122
00:06:06,621 --> 00:06:08,620
because the steam is used by the engine.

123
00:06:08,621 --> 00:06:09,860
used by the engine.

124
00:06:09,960 --> 00:06:12,620
It goes back up into the condenser, which

125
00:06:12,720 --> 00:06:15,020
looks like a radiator, but it's a condenser.

126
00:06:15,120 --> 00:06:17,420
The cool air from the fan cools the steam,

127
00:06:17,520 --> 00:06:21,060
turns it into water, and you use it over and over again.

128
00:06:21,160 --> 00:06:23,560
I mean, eventually, it dissipates,

129
00:06:23,660 --> 00:06:27,080
whereas a Stanley, you might get only a mile and a half

130
00:06:27,180 --> 00:06:28,380
on a gallon of water.

131
00:06:28,480 --> 00:06:32,080
And this, you might get five miles on a gallon of water.

132
00:06:32,180 --> 00:06:34,680
The ride may have been quiet and comfortable,

133
00:06:34,780 --> 00:06:37,880
but the lower sticker prices on gasoline-powered cars,

134
00:06:37,881 --> 00:06:42,120
along with the quicker starting internal combustion engines,

135
00:06:42,220 --> 00:06:44,720
caused steamer sales to fizzle.

136
00:06:44,820 --> 00:06:48,920
Gasoline cars could do it quicker, better, faster.

137
00:06:49,020 --> 00:06:51,020
And that's really the name of the game.

138
00:06:51,120 --> 00:06:53,920
I love them, but if your wife's pregnant,

139
00:06:54,020 --> 00:06:55,240
you need to get to the hospital.

140
00:06:55,340 --> 00:06:56,740
You can't be out there with a match and a,

141
00:06:56,840 --> 00:06:58,440
I'll be in another 10 minutes on it.

142
00:06:58,540 --> 00:07:01,340
It's not going to work.

143
00:07:01,440 --> 00:07:03,840
Decades before steam cars, and for decades

144
00:07:03,940 --> 00:07:07,880
after their fall, steam locomotives ruled the rails.

145
00:07:07,881 --> 00:07:11,120
In 1804, English inventor Richard Trevithick

146
00:07:11,220 --> 00:07:14,520
unveiled a steam locomotive capable of hauling 10 tons.

147
00:07:17,760 --> 00:07:20,020
They quickly grew in size and power

148
00:07:20,120 --> 00:07:24,200
and became important workhorses in the Industrial Revolution.

149
00:07:24,300 --> 00:07:26,800
Throughout the 1800s, the steam locomotive

150
00:07:26,900 --> 00:07:30,600
played a major role in the settlement of the American West.

151
00:07:30,700 --> 00:07:33,600
The defining moment occurred in 1869,

152
00:07:33,700 --> 00:07:35,980
when the completion of the transcontinental railroad

153
00:07:35,981 --> 00:07:37,480
from coast to coast.

154
00:07:40,480 --> 00:07:43,880
In remote Eatley, Nevada, the Nevada Northern Railway

155
00:07:43,980 --> 00:07:45,920
houses its historic steam locomotives

156
00:07:46,020 --> 00:07:49,320
in their original engine house.

157
00:07:49,420 --> 00:07:51,820
The locomotive we're going to take out today

158
00:07:51,920 --> 00:07:54,140
is locomotive 93.

159
00:07:54,240 --> 00:07:59,140
She was purchased by the railroad in July of 1909.

160
00:07:59,240 --> 00:08:02,140
Right now, the fireman is preparing the fire

161
00:08:02,240 --> 00:08:03,640
to produce the steam.

162
00:08:05,980 --> 00:08:12,980
What I'm doing now is I'm shoveling coal into the firebox and we cover the grates evenly

163
00:08:14,980 --> 00:08:20,475
with fire and build the pressure up for operating pressure about 185 PSI.

164
00:08:20,476 --> 00:08:21,980
This firebox is big.

165
00:08:26,980 --> 00:08:30,639
It's about 12 feet deep and about 6 feet wide.

166
00:08:30,640 --> 00:08:33,980
This engine takes a lot of coal to make it

167
00:08:33,981 --> 00:08:34,980
run.

168
00:08:34,981 --> 00:08:39,980
It's an incredibly labor-intensive machine right now.

169
00:08:40,080 --> 00:08:44,820
We're lubing it, getting it ready to go out on the line.

170
00:08:44,920 --> 00:08:47,620
Once the boiler builds up enough pressure,

171
00:08:47,720 --> 00:08:50,620
steam generates electricity for the train's lights

172
00:08:50,720 --> 00:08:53,240
and runs the air pumps on the front of the locomotive

173
00:08:53,340 --> 00:08:57,240
that produce compressed air for braking and other functions.

174
00:08:57,340 --> 00:08:59,440
Then up goes the engine house door,

175
00:08:59,540 --> 00:09:02,040
and locomotive 93 slowly chugs out.

176
00:09:04,980 --> 00:09:18,980
We just received the highball, releasing the engine brake, placing the power reverse and

177
00:09:24,160 --> 00:09:28,220
forward and pull it out on the throttle and two blasts on the whistle.

178
00:09:28,221 --> 00:09:30,660
And off we go.

179
00:09:30,661 --> 00:09:37,660
This is copper mining country, and for nearly 50 years,

180
00:09:37,760 --> 00:09:42,600
locomotive number 93's job was to haul ore cars.

181
00:09:42,700 --> 00:09:44,100
This is a freight locomotive.

182
00:09:44,200 --> 00:09:45,800
You can tell by the smaller wheels.

183
00:09:45,900 --> 00:09:48,620
It has a lot of track of effort, but very little horsepower.

184
00:09:48,720 --> 00:09:51,320
Track of effort's about 42,000 pounds,

185
00:09:51,420 --> 00:09:54,320
but the horsepower is only 905.

186
00:09:54,420 --> 00:09:56,460
It's built for power, not for speed.

187
00:09:56,560 --> 00:09:59,460
It used to pull about 33 ore cars up the hill.

188
00:09:59,461 --> 00:10:00,460
up the hill.

189
00:10:03,960 --> 00:10:05,460
Since this is a working locomotive,

190
00:10:05,461 --> 00:10:06,960
you got to be careful to let you touch.

191
00:10:06,961 --> 00:10:08,460
Everything's extremely hot.

192
00:10:12,460 --> 00:10:15,460
Before they return number 93 to the engine house,

193
00:10:15,461 --> 00:10:18,160
the crew performs a boiler cleaning procedure

194
00:10:18,161 --> 00:10:20,660
that gives just a hint of the amount of pressurized steam

195
00:10:20,661 --> 00:10:22,960
inside.

196
00:10:22,961 --> 00:10:25,760
After each run, we do what we call a blowdown.

197
00:10:25,761 --> 00:10:28,260
We do this because sediments build up in the boiler,

198
00:10:28,261 --> 00:10:30,260
And we have to clean the boiler out,

199
00:10:30,360 --> 00:10:31,900
get those sediments out of the boiler.

200
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:32,800
So here goes.

201
00:10:41,100 --> 00:10:43,300
The Nevada Northern Railway hasn't preserved

202
00:10:43,400 --> 00:10:46,820
only steam locomotives.

203
00:10:46,920 --> 00:10:51,180
This 100-ton steam crane is called the Big Hook.

204
00:10:51,280 --> 00:10:55,080
Built in 1907, its main purpose was to clear train wrecks

205
00:10:55,081 --> 00:10:58,380
and place derailed cars back on the tracks.

206
00:10:58,480 --> 00:11:01,720
This steam-powered crane is similar to a steam locomotive,

207
00:11:01,820 --> 00:11:05,620
except that we're not running wheels on steel rails.

208
00:11:05,720 --> 00:11:10,720
What we're running here are large gears that pick up the boom

209
00:11:10,820 --> 00:11:13,940
and also the two hooks in the front.

210
00:11:14,040 --> 00:11:16,240
Before it can pick up a derailed car,

211
00:11:16,340 --> 00:11:18,940
the crane's outriggers must be extended.

212
00:11:19,040 --> 00:11:21,680
Then the crew places wood blocks underneath each

213
00:11:21,780 --> 00:11:23,740
to stabilize the crane.

214
00:11:23,741 --> 00:11:27,740
Now the heavy lifting can begin.

215
00:11:27,840 --> 00:11:30,740
Next, we're going to swing the crane over to the load

216
00:11:30,840 --> 00:11:32,180
we're going to pick up.

217
00:11:32,280 --> 00:11:35,380
And to do that, I have to engage the swing hook.

218
00:11:35,480 --> 00:11:37,880
And then I have to put a direction on it.

219
00:11:37,980 --> 00:11:41,900
And then I will apply steam, which is powered by these

220
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:42,700
levers.

221
00:11:42,800 --> 00:11:43,600
And here we go.

222
00:11:49,200 --> 00:11:53,100
Next, we're going to lower the hooks to pick up the load, which

223
00:11:53,101 --> 00:11:54,400
which would mean that I engage.

224
00:11:54,500 --> 00:11:57,400
First, I engage this one, this one here, this clutch.

225
00:12:00,400 --> 00:12:01,400
That is engaged.

226
00:12:01,500 --> 00:12:04,740
Now we're going to let her down.

227
00:12:04,840 --> 00:12:07,740
The big hook may look huge, and it's brawny enough

228
00:12:07,840 --> 00:12:08,840
to lift up this ore car.

229
00:12:12,560 --> 00:12:15,460
But it's dwarfed by the enormity of the steamships built

230
00:12:15,560 --> 00:12:18,760
across the oceans.

231
00:12:18,860 --> 00:12:21,820
The Titanic was the largest when it sank on its maiden voyage

232
00:12:21,821 --> 00:12:24,460
in 1912.

233
00:12:24,560 --> 00:12:28,060
But the RMS Queen Elizabeth, launched in 1938,

234
00:12:28,160 --> 00:12:31,260
was the biggest steamship ever built.

235
00:12:31,360 --> 00:12:33,860
We know the smaller vessels designed to navigate rivers

236
00:12:33,960 --> 00:12:37,060
and lakes as steamboats.

237
00:12:37,160 --> 00:12:41,380
It was in New York in 1807 that Robert Fulton's Claremont

238
00:12:41,480 --> 00:12:44,080
offered the first successful commercial steamboat service

239
00:12:44,180 --> 00:12:45,680
in the world.

240
00:12:45,780 --> 00:12:50,420
Today, there are only a few working steamboats in America.

241
00:12:50,421 --> 00:12:52,720
In New Orleans, the Natchez provides a reminder

242
00:12:52,820 --> 00:12:57,260
of the golden age of steamboating on the Mississippi.

243
00:12:57,360 --> 00:13:01,560
While the boat is a recreation built in 1975,

244
00:13:01,660 --> 00:13:03,600
the enormous steam engines are hand-me-downs

245
00:13:03,700 --> 00:13:06,240
from a bygone era.

246
00:13:06,340 --> 00:13:10,480
These engines that you see right here were built in 1925.

247
00:13:10,580 --> 00:13:13,240
They were built for the steamboat Clarity, which

248
00:13:13,340 --> 00:13:17,420
was a pushboat for the US Steel Company in Pennsylvania.

249
00:13:17,421 --> 00:13:21,660
It wasn't unusual for steamboats to get machinery

250
00:13:21,760 --> 00:13:23,460
from the previous vessel.

251
00:13:23,560 --> 00:13:25,960
The wooden boats only lasted a short time,

252
00:13:26,060 --> 00:13:27,460
and the machinery would quite often

253
00:13:27,560 --> 00:13:31,160
be removed from one older vessel onto the new one.

254
00:13:31,260 --> 00:13:34,380
Both engines work in tandem to move the huge arms that

255
00:13:34,480 --> 00:13:38,380
turn the 26-ton oak and steel paddle wheel.

256
00:13:38,480 --> 00:13:40,780
The total maximum output of the two engines

257
00:13:40,880 --> 00:13:44,580
is equivalent to about 1,600 horsepower.

258
00:13:44,680 --> 00:13:46,180
The boilers that supply the steam

259
00:13:46,181 --> 00:13:48,620
vintage equipment because of safety, economic,

260
00:13:48,720 --> 00:13:50,920
and environmental reasons.

261
00:13:51,020 --> 00:13:52,720
The old boats use coal.

262
00:13:52,820 --> 00:13:55,020
Unlike us, we're using diesel fuel.

263
00:13:55,120 --> 00:13:56,120
There are two boilers.

264
00:13:56,220 --> 00:13:58,920
We only use one at a time.

265
00:13:59,020 --> 00:14:01,960
Not all the steam produced heads right to the engines.

266
00:14:02,060 --> 00:14:04,540
One small pipe goes up to the top of the boat.

267
00:14:08,440 --> 00:14:10,140
The few boats that are in service now

268
00:14:10,240 --> 00:14:11,840
have a very distinctive whistle.

269
00:14:11,940 --> 00:14:14,540
Most steamboat people can automatically hear them

270
00:14:14,541 --> 00:14:16,940
and know what exact boat they're from.

271
00:14:17,040 --> 00:14:18,440
A whistle isn't the only way

272
00:14:18,540 --> 00:14:20,840
to get people's attention on this boat.

273
00:14:20,940 --> 00:14:23,040
Another pipe runs across the top deck

274
00:14:23,140 --> 00:14:25,980
to supply steam to the calliope.

275
00:14:26,080 --> 00:14:29,380
First thing you have to do is clear the condensation

276
00:14:29,480 --> 00:14:32,300
that's built up in the whistles.

277
00:14:32,400 --> 00:14:34,600
We have 32 keys on our keyboard.

278
00:14:34,700 --> 00:14:38,000
Each key corresponds to a steam whistle.

279
00:14:38,100 --> 00:14:41,300
The steam organ was patented by Joshua C. Stoddard

280
00:14:41,301 --> 00:14:44,700
of Worcester, Massachusetts in 1856.

281
00:14:44,701 --> 00:14:46,900
His brother was a riverboat captain.

282
00:14:46,901 --> 00:14:49,540
He gave it to his brother who put it on the boat,

283
00:14:49,541 --> 00:14:52,540
and it became a principal means of advertising

284
00:14:52,541 --> 00:14:55,140
and welcoming passengers aboard steamboats

285
00:14:55,141 --> 00:14:56,940
by the late 1850s.

286
00:14:56,941 --> 00:15:00,860
It was later named Calliope for the Greek news of voice.

287
00:15:02,860 --> 00:15:04,860
I'm going to close this act with a theme

288
00:15:04,861 --> 00:15:07,300
you should all recognize.

289
00:15:11,300 --> 00:15:15,780
Steam can and did directly power many forms of transportation,

290
00:15:15,880 --> 00:15:18,600
but it also once powered factories,

291
00:15:18,700 --> 00:15:21,440
and Jay Leno's got the steam engines to prove it.

292
00:15:21,540 --> 00:15:24,480
If you like watching mechanical things move,

293
00:15:24,580 --> 00:15:27,380
it doesn't get any more interesting than this.

294
00:15:27,480 --> 00:15:31,480
Between 1941 and 1944, Union Pacific

295
00:15:31,580 --> 00:15:35,160
manufactured 25 big boys, the largest steam locomotives

296
00:15:35,260 --> 00:15:36,620
ever built.

297
00:15:36,720 --> 00:15:39,860
They were 132 feet long and weighed 1.5 feet

298
00:15:39,861 --> 00:15:43,160
and weighed 1.2 million pounds.

299
00:15:43,260 --> 00:15:44,860
None are operational today.

300
00:15:53,600 --> 00:15:59,220
Trains, boats, and steam cars created a more mobile world.

301
00:16:02,320 --> 00:16:05,320
But just as important was steam's role in the factories

302
00:16:05,420 --> 00:16:07,660
of the Industrial Revolution.

303
00:16:07,661 --> 00:16:10,660
Jay Leno decided to go back 150 years

304
00:16:10,760 --> 00:16:14,660
and see what some old but proven technology had to offer.

305
00:16:14,760 --> 00:16:17,700
He acquired a Wright stationary steam engine,

306
00:16:17,800 --> 00:16:20,300
built in the 1860s, and installed it

307
00:16:20,400 --> 00:16:21,800
in his huge automotive garage.

308
00:16:21,900 --> 00:16:24,720
Abraham Lincoln was president when this thing was designed.

309
00:16:24,820 --> 00:16:26,420
It weighs 11 tons.

310
00:16:26,520 --> 00:16:29,220
This flywheel here is three tons.

311
00:16:29,320 --> 00:16:30,420
It's built in two pieces.

312
00:16:30,520 --> 00:16:34,220
But the rest of it is all one casting.

313
00:16:34,320 --> 00:16:36,660
Of course, if you decide to buy a steam engine,

314
00:16:36,661 --> 00:16:39,660
you also need a source of steam.

315
00:16:39,760 --> 00:16:42,360
We've got a modern Clayton steam generator.

316
00:16:42,460 --> 00:16:43,660
It's a little complicated.

317
00:16:43,760 --> 00:16:44,660
Bernard has the cheat sheet.

318
00:16:44,760 --> 00:16:46,160
Get the cheat sheet, Bernard.

319
00:16:46,260 --> 00:16:48,300
We always want to make sure we fire this thing right up

320
00:16:48,400 --> 00:16:50,400
properly because there's a lot of valves to open

321
00:16:50,500 --> 00:16:51,600
and things you have to do.

322
00:16:51,700 --> 00:16:53,420
Well, first step, we've got to check the water

323
00:16:53,520 --> 00:16:54,920
and the header tank up there.

324
00:16:55,020 --> 00:16:56,220
The header tank looks good.

325
00:16:56,320 --> 00:16:57,520
We got water in the header tank.

326
00:16:57,620 --> 00:16:58,520
We got it on.

327
00:16:58,620 --> 00:16:59,620
OK.

328
00:16:59,720 --> 00:17:00,720
Next, Jay and Bernard open a series of valves

329
00:17:00,820 --> 00:17:03,460
to get the water flowing through the system.

330
00:17:03,560 --> 00:17:05,620
So that's starting to look pretty good there.

331
00:17:05,621 --> 00:17:08,120
So let's push the fill run button over there

332
00:17:08,121 --> 00:17:10,120
and we'll get some fire going.

333
00:17:10,121 --> 00:17:11,620
Right, Captain!

334
00:17:11,621 --> 00:17:14,620
Now we're waiting for steam to come out of that pipe up on top.

335
00:17:16,620 --> 00:17:18,620
There she blows, Captain!

336
00:17:20,620 --> 00:17:22,620
Now that we got steam, let's go back inside.

337
00:17:22,621 --> 00:17:25,620
Luckily, this thing operates on natural gas,

338
00:17:25,621 --> 00:17:27,620
so I don't have to shovel coal all day.

339
00:17:27,621 --> 00:17:29,620
That wouldn't do it.

340
00:17:31,620 --> 00:17:34,620
As you can see, we've got almost 140 pounds of steam pressure,

341
00:17:34,621 --> 00:17:37,160
much more than this would have run on at the time.

342
00:17:37,260 --> 00:17:40,320
So let's open our valve here and see what happens.

343
00:17:40,420 --> 00:17:43,660
And everything starts moving.

344
00:17:43,760 --> 00:17:46,060
I still got a kick out of this each time it starts.

345
00:17:46,160 --> 00:17:47,360
It's always amazing to me.

346
00:17:47,460 --> 00:17:49,840
You know, we're so used to reee and machines,

347
00:17:49,940 --> 00:17:50,800
starting machines.

348
00:17:50,900 --> 00:17:54,440
The fact that this runs so quietly.

349
00:17:54,540 --> 00:17:57,340
One reason this 11-ton single-piston right steam

350
00:17:57,440 --> 00:18:00,740
engine does run so quietly is that Jay installed a 7-ton,

351
00:18:00,840 --> 00:18:04,620
perfectly level cement base for the engine to rest on.

352
00:18:04,621 --> 00:18:08,420
If you're a little off, your piston will eat into the bore

353
00:18:08,520 --> 00:18:09,920
and wear your cylinder out.

354
00:18:10,020 --> 00:18:12,860
So that's the real trick, making sure everything is level

355
00:18:12,960 --> 00:18:15,860
and smooth and moves freely.

356
00:18:15,960 --> 00:18:19,060
So far, Jay hasn't put his steam engine to work.

357
00:18:19,160 --> 00:18:21,840
Back in the steam age, the rotating flywheel would run

358
00:18:21,940 --> 00:18:25,200
a big belt to transfer its power to saws or other mill

359
00:18:25,300 --> 00:18:26,440
or factory machinery.

360
00:18:28,340 --> 00:18:30,520
This is what the Luddites were afraid of.

361
00:18:30,620 --> 00:18:32,680
You all heard the expression, Luddites, people don't like

362
00:18:32,780 --> 00:18:33,880
technology.

363
00:18:33,881 --> 00:18:37,720
But you realize a machine like this would probably replace

364
00:18:37,820 --> 00:18:39,180
100 men working.

365
00:18:39,280 --> 00:18:42,920
It was 125 horsepower, which doesn't seem like much now.

366
00:18:43,020 --> 00:18:45,420
We have engines this big to make 125 horsepower.

367
00:18:45,520 --> 00:18:47,920
But back in the day, this was almost

368
00:18:48,020 --> 00:18:50,640
like space shuttle technology.

369
00:18:50,740 --> 00:18:52,440
What makes old steam engines fascinating

370
00:18:52,540 --> 00:18:57,740
in this age of electronics are the rhythmic sounds

371
00:18:57,840 --> 00:19:00,840
and the enormous moving parts, a potential hazard

372
00:19:00,940 --> 00:19:03,240
in 19th century factories.

373
00:19:03,241 --> 00:19:04,980
There was no OSHA back in the day.

374
00:19:05,080 --> 00:19:07,180
There was no health and safety standards.

375
00:19:07,280 --> 00:19:09,180
The guy would go, hey, I'll fix it.

376
00:19:09,280 --> 00:19:11,480
And that was pretty much the end of it.

377
00:19:11,580 --> 00:19:15,980
Next, Jay starts up his even older 1832 steam engine.

378
00:19:16,080 --> 00:19:18,980
Give it a little bit of a push here to get it going.

379
00:19:19,080 --> 00:19:20,900
Get it over center.

380
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:21,900
And there she goes.

381
00:19:25,500 --> 00:19:27,640
As you can see, there's kind of a quiet elegance

382
00:19:27,740 --> 00:19:28,700
to these things.

383
00:19:28,800 --> 00:19:30,600
This is what they call a walking beam,

384
00:19:30,601 --> 00:19:33,100
because it's a beam and it looks like it's walking.

385
00:19:33,200 --> 00:19:35,100
It's pretty self-explanatory.

386
00:19:35,200 --> 00:19:38,100
This ran a color and dye factory in England

387
00:19:38,200 --> 00:19:39,600
back in the early 1800s.

388
00:19:39,700 --> 00:19:42,640
And I'm told this engine ran for almost 100 years

389
00:19:42,740 --> 00:19:45,640
continuously, just like this, chug, chug, chug.

390
00:19:45,740 --> 00:19:47,960
That's what it did.

391
00:19:48,060 --> 00:19:50,160
The engine isn't entirely original,

392
00:19:50,260 --> 00:19:52,460
but there's a good reason the oilers were added later

393
00:19:52,560 --> 00:19:54,860
to make lubrication easier.

394
00:19:54,960 --> 00:19:56,360
This is 1832.

395
00:19:56,460 --> 00:19:57,600
How did they lubricate them?

396
00:19:57,601 --> 00:20:01,100
What you do is you'd bring a pig in, they'd slaughter a pig,

397
00:20:01,200 --> 00:20:04,100
they'd rub the greasy parts all over the metal,

398
00:20:04,200 --> 00:20:05,400
run it for a while.

399
00:20:05,500 --> 00:20:07,280
Hey, we need another pig, bring another pig in,

400
00:20:07,380 --> 00:20:09,980
they'd slaughter another pig, rub the greasy parts all over,

401
00:20:10,080 --> 00:20:11,840
and probably ate a lot of pork chops.

402
00:20:11,940 --> 00:20:13,040
That's probably what they did.

403
00:20:13,140 --> 00:20:15,260
Luckily, we don't do that anymore.

404
00:20:15,360 --> 00:20:17,960
Centrifugal force causes these circulating balls

405
00:20:18,060 --> 00:20:19,960
to flare outward.

406
00:20:20,060 --> 00:20:22,400
They're the engine's main safety feature.

407
00:20:22,500 --> 00:20:25,160
The faster you go, the higher this rises,

408
00:20:25,161 --> 00:20:28,000
which moves that lever, which controls your throttle.

409
00:20:28,001 --> 00:20:30,160
You hear the expression balls out.

410
00:20:30,161 --> 00:20:32,160
It doesn't mean what you think, okay?

411
00:20:32,161 --> 00:20:34,160
What it means is your ball's running all the way out,

412
00:20:34,161 --> 00:20:36,160
your engine is running at full speed,

413
00:20:36,161 --> 00:20:39,160
and you keep it from over-revving it and breaking your motor.

414
00:20:39,161 --> 00:20:41,160
It essentially works as a governor.

415
00:20:41,161 --> 00:20:44,160
It limits how much throttle you can put into it.

416
00:20:44,161 --> 00:20:47,160
Jay's engines may be antiques,

417
00:20:47,161 --> 00:20:50,160
but steam is still a vital source of power.

418
00:20:50,161 --> 00:20:54,160
All your nuclear subs run on steam.

419
00:20:54,161 --> 00:20:55,160
steam.

420
00:20:55,260 --> 00:20:59,100
Steam turbines run a lot of power plants.

421
00:20:59,200 --> 00:21:02,800
Steam is still a force to be reckoned with.

422
00:21:02,900 --> 00:21:07,200
Cold, natural gas, and nuclear energy all create steam.

423
00:21:07,300 --> 00:21:09,140
But there's a more environmentally friendly way

424
00:21:09,240 --> 00:21:13,140
to turn the turbines that generate electricity.

425
00:21:13,240 --> 00:21:16,720
Yellowstone National Park spurting and gushing geysers

426
00:21:16,820 --> 00:21:19,520
are examples of the volatile subterranean interaction

427
00:21:19,620 --> 00:21:21,160
of heat and water.

428
00:21:21,260 --> 00:21:24,160
Another is the biggest steam explosion in US history.

429
00:21:24,161 --> 00:21:28,260
The cataclysmic eruption of Mount St. Helens in Washington

430
00:21:28,360 --> 00:21:31,500
State on May 18, 1980.

431
00:21:31,600 --> 00:21:34,900
Inside the volcano, water hit superheated rock

432
00:21:35,000 --> 00:21:37,800
and flashed into steam with the explosive force

433
00:21:37,900 --> 00:21:40,920
of about 20 million tons of TNT.

434
00:21:41,020 --> 00:21:46,220
The blast devastated 150 square miles of forest in six minutes.

435
00:21:46,320 --> 00:21:50,120
That's the power of steam uncontrolled.

436
00:21:50,220 --> 00:21:52,320
Elsewhere, that natural power is being harnessed

437
00:21:52,321 --> 00:21:55,920
to produce electricity. The first geothermal power plants

438
00:21:56,020 --> 00:22:00,760
in the US were built in 1962 at the Geyser's dry steam field

439
00:22:00,860 --> 00:22:04,260
located in Northern California.

440
00:22:04,360 --> 00:22:06,440
It's still the largest producing geothermal field

441
00:22:06,540 --> 00:22:10,300
in the world and is one of only two locations that use high

442
00:22:10,400 --> 00:22:13,040
temperature dry steam as the resource to generate

443
00:22:13,140 --> 00:22:17,280
electricity. In dry steam power plants,

444
00:22:17,380 --> 00:22:21,380
the steam and no water shoots up from the wells and is used to

445
00:22:21,381 --> 00:22:25,380
and is used to turn turbines, while most other geothermal

446
00:22:25,480 --> 00:22:28,320
plants, called flash plants, use naturally occurring

447
00:22:28,420 --> 00:22:30,480
superheated water as a source of steam.

448
00:22:33,420 --> 00:22:35,520
In Southern California's Imperial Valley,

449
00:22:35,620 --> 00:22:38,340
along the shore of the Salton Sea,

450
00:22:38,440 --> 00:22:42,740
the Cal Energy Company currently has 10 flash plants, all

451
00:22:42,840 --> 00:22:46,040
situated over a hotbed of geothermal activity.

452
00:22:46,140 --> 00:22:49,040
On those 10 plants, we're generating 340 megawatts

453
00:22:49,041 --> 00:22:53,040
which equates to about 333,000 homes that it powers.

454
00:22:53,140 --> 00:22:55,440
And we sell on long-term power contracts

455
00:22:55,540 --> 00:22:59,680
to utilities throughout Southern California and Arizona.

456
00:22:59,780 --> 00:23:01,780
Just like the Salton Sea above,

457
00:23:01,880 --> 00:23:04,580
the water below the ground is very salty.

458
00:23:04,680 --> 00:23:06,420
It's referred to as brine.

459
00:23:08,360 --> 00:23:11,300
Geothermal production wells, similar to oil wells,

460
00:23:11,400 --> 00:23:15,340
drill down thousands of feet to tap into the more than 360

461
00:23:15,341 --> 00:23:18,640
360-degree Fahrenheit reservoirs of brine trapped

462
00:23:18,740 --> 00:23:19,580
in the bedrock.

463
00:23:21,740 --> 00:23:23,880
Tremendous pressure causes the superheated brine

464
00:23:23,980 --> 00:23:24,980
to rush to the surface.

465
00:23:28,280 --> 00:23:30,880
This is Vonderay 1 production well.

466
00:23:30,980 --> 00:23:33,820
Vonderay 1 was just recognized by the World Geothermal

467
00:23:33,920 --> 00:23:37,360
Resources Council as being the most prolific geothermal well

468
00:23:37,460 --> 00:23:38,360
in the world.

469
00:23:38,460 --> 00:23:41,860
We currently get about 2.3 million pounds per hour

470
00:23:41,960 --> 00:23:45,340
of brine from here, which in our plant we could make

471
00:23:45,341 --> 00:23:47,140
worth of power.

472
00:23:47,240 --> 00:23:49,180
Don't let all that noise scare them.

473
00:23:49,280 --> 00:23:52,440
These yellow bars are actually restraints

474
00:23:52,540 --> 00:23:54,680
because the well head was moving so much,

475
00:23:54,780 --> 00:23:58,780
and it was a little too prolific.

476
00:23:58,880 --> 00:24:01,580
The hot brine from the wells flows through pipes

477
00:24:01,680 --> 00:24:04,300
into large cylindrical tanks.

478
00:24:04,400 --> 00:24:07,800
This part of the process is why this type of geothermal plant

479
00:24:07,900 --> 00:24:09,700
is called a flash plant.

480
00:24:09,800 --> 00:24:12,800
As the pressure is lowered inside the vessel,

481
00:24:12,801 --> 00:24:16,640
steam is flashed similar to what you would see in a pressure

482
00:24:16,740 --> 00:24:18,380
cooker in your home.

483
00:24:18,480 --> 00:24:21,280
As the steam is flashed, it's routed from the top

484
00:24:21,380 --> 00:24:22,740
of the vessel.

485
00:24:22,840 --> 00:24:27,820
The brine water that remains is exiting on the bottom half

486
00:24:27,920 --> 00:24:31,320
so that there is no contamination between the two.

487
00:24:31,420 --> 00:24:33,320
The steam that contains no salts

488
00:24:33,420 --> 00:24:36,160
is then routed to turn the fan-like blades inside the

489
00:24:36,260 --> 00:24:39,860
turbines that generate electricity.

490
00:24:39,861 --> 00:24:42,260
While the steam is used to turn the turbines,

491
00:24:42,360 --> 00:24:44,500
the remaining liquid that didn't flash into steam

492
00:24:44,600 --> 00:24:48,100
is pumped away from the plants to injection wells that recycle

493
00:24:48,200 --> 00:24:51,800
it back into the underground reservoirs.

494
00:24:51,900 --> 00:24:54,600
We re-inject 80% of all the fluid

495
00:24:54,700 --> 00:24:56,620
that we pull out in the production system,

496
00:24:56,720 --> 00:25:00,120
so we're self-sustaining the reservoir.

497
00:25:00,220 --> 00:25:02,140
Geologists estimate there's probably

498
00:25:02,240 --> 00:25:06,520
another 2,300 megawatts of power still untapped in the area,

499
00:25:06,521 --> 00:25:09,760
enough to supply 2 million more homes.

500
00:25:09,860 --> 00:25:12,560
Cal Energy is planning to build new plants,

501
00:25:12,660 --> 00:25:14,560
but new power lines will need to be approved

502
00:25:14,660 --> 00:25:17,560
to carry the additional electricity to feed hungry

503
00:25:17,660 --> 00:25:19,560
cities.

504
00:25:19,660 --> 00:25:21,560
While naturally occurring steam is being used

505
00:25:21,660 --> 00:25:25,980
to generate electricity, what have you got to clean this?

506
00:25:26,080 --> 00:25:28,980
It's time to let loose the power of man-made steam.

507
00:25:32,120 --> 00:25:36,020
In 1876, the 70-foot-tall 1,400 horse

508
00:25:36,021 --> 00:25:39,220
coreless steam engine was the largest ever built.

509
00:25:39,320 --> 00:25:43,620
After 30 years of service, the 650-ton engine sold for scrap

510
00:25:43,720 --> 00:25:45,460
at just $5,200.

511
00:25:53,380 --> 00:25:56,080
They're the heavy lifters and big movers.

512
00:25:56,180 --> 00:25:59,580
But they get gunky and grimy and need to be cleaned.

513
00:25:59,680 --> 00:26:01,380
So what can you do?

514
00:26:01,480 --> 00:26:04,480
The Sioux Corporation of South Dakota has the answer.

515
00:26:04,481 --> 00:26:08,520
roll out one of its steam cleaning units, attach a water

516
00:26:08,620 --> 00:26:12,520
supply, fire up the boiler, and blast away.

517
00:26:17,360 --> 00:26:21,200
Steam is just a fantastic way to remove grease and dirt

518
00:26:21,300 --> 00:26:23,200
from heavy equipment.

519
00:26:23,300 --> 00:26:25,600
This steam cleaning unit has a diesel tank

520
00:26:25,700 --> 00:26:27,600
that supplies fuel to the burner.

521
00:26:27,700 --> 00:26:30,580
The upper portion is called a water tube boiler.

522
00:26:30,680 --> 00:26:33,680
Hot air produced by the burners below circulates around the

523
00:26:33,681 --> 00:26:36,980
around two coils of water-filled steel pipe, up

524
00:26:37,080 --> 00:26:40,220
through the center, down between the coils, then up

525
00:26:40,320 --> 00:26:44,020
the outside before exhausting out of the top of the unit.

526
00:26:44,120 --> 00:26:47,220
To build one of these coils, we take 200 feet of pipe

527
00:26:47,320 --> 00:26:51,040
and wind it on a drum into a cylinder.

528
00:26:51,140 --> 00:26:53,940
And then we wind a smaller one for the inner cylinder of

529
00:26:54,040 --> 00:26:58,080
another 100 feet for a total of 300 feet of pipe that's all

530
00:26:58,180 --> 00:27:01,780
wound together and welded together to have one continuous

531
00:27:01,781 --> 00:27:03,680
pass of 300 feet.

532
00:27:03,780 --> 00:27:06,720
When the water passes through this 300 feet of pipe,

533
00:27:06,820 --> 00:27:10,120
it starts out at 60 degrees Fahrenheit and ends up at 325

534
00:27:10,220 --> 00:27:12,220
degrees Fahrenheit.

535
00:27:12,320 --> 00:27:14,760
The hot pressurized water flows out of the boiler

536
00:27:14,860 --> 00:27:17,300
