00:33:14,100 --> 00:33:17,940
We take 3,700 gallons per hour of fuel oil,

659
00:33:18,040 --> 00:33:20,240
and we create a fireball inside the boiler,

660
00:33:20,340 --> 00:33:24,340
which heats up 60,000 gallons per hour of water,

661
00:33:24,440 --> 00:33:28,060
which goes up to the drum that flashes into steam.

662
00:33:28,160 --> 00:33:29,760
Each of our three high-pressure boilers

663
00:33:29,860 --> 00:33:31,260
has two steam drums.

664
00:33:31,360 --> 00:33:35,260
They produce approximately 450,000 pounds of steam per hour

665
00:33:35,360 --> 00:33:37,560
at a temperature of 800 degrees.

666
00:33:37,561 --> 00:33:40,300
Each drum extends back 25 to 30 feet.

667
00:33:40,400 --> 00:33:42,860
So you're only seeing the face of it right here.

668
00:33:42,960 --> 00:33:45,260
In a central control room, workers carefully

669
00:33:45,360 --> 00:33:47,640
monitor both the steam production process

670
00:33:47,740 --> 00:33:49,740
and the delivery system.

671
00:33:49,840 --> 00:33:53,680
We produce steam at 800 psi and a high temperature

672
00:33:53,780 --> 00:33:56,180
of 840 degrees.

673
00:33:56,280 --> 00:33:58,680
For the safety of the steam system unit,

674
00:33:58,780 --> 00:34:03,920
we reduce that pressure to 200 psi and 410 degrees,

675
00:34:04,020 --> 00:34:06,820
which we send out in the underground pipes.

676
00:34:06,821 --> 00:34:08,320
This is our steam mimic system.

677
00:34:08,420 --> 00:34:11,900
This system monitors over 105 miles of piping

678
00:34:12,000 --> 00:34:13,200
in the steam system.

679
00:34:13,300 --> 00:34:15,160
It monitors the pressure in the steam system.

680
00:34:15,260 --> 00:34:17,700
So if there is a problem in anywhere in the system

681
00:34:17,800 --> 00:34:19,560
throughout Manhattan, we'll know immediately

682
00:34:19,660 --> 00:34:21,700
where that problem is, and we can dispatch crews there

683
00:34:21,800 --> 00:34:24,600
to correct the problem.

684
00:34:24,700 --> 00:34:28,300
Con Edison has been in the steam business since 1930.

685
00:34:28,400 --> 00:34:31,240
The underground pipe responsible for this explosion

686
00:34:31,340 --> 00:34:33,720
was laid in 1923.

687
00:34:33,820 --> 00:34:36,520
It was part of the preexisting steam system Con Edison

688
00:34:36,521 --> 00:34:39,280
purchased that first began supplying steam to Manhattan

689
00:34:39,380 --> 00:34:42,660
customers in 1882.

690
00:34:42,760 --> 00:34:45,860
So does that mean steam is outdated?

691
00:34:45,960 --> 00:34:48,760
The Hearst Tower, completed in 2006

692
00:34:48,860 --> 00:34:51,060
and considered technologically and environmentally

693
00:34:51,160 --> 00:34:54,880
state of the art, is one of Con Edison's newer customers.

694
00:34:54,980 --> 00:34:56,880
We're in the basement of the Hearst Tower,

695
00:34:56,980 --> 00:34:59,780
where the steam from the Con Edison plant comes in through

696
00:34:59,880 --> 00:35:05,780
the street in this pipe at 175 pounds per square inch.

697
00:35:05,781 --> 00:35:08,980
It then gets reduced through the control panel here

698
00:35:09,080 --> 00:35:13,780
to 60 pounds, ultimately getting reduced to 15 pounds.

699
00:35:13,880 --> 00:35:17,020
And it then supplies all the heat and hot water

700
00:35:17,120 --> 00:35:19,020
to the building.

701
00:35:19,120 --> 00:35:21,900
Inside the building's 10-story high atrium,

702
00:35:22,000 --> 00:35:25,500
there are no old-fashioned radiators or hot air vents.

703
00:35:25,600 --> 00:35:27,300
Instead, the heat derived from the steam

704
00:35:27,400 --> 00:35:29,740
flows in tubes under the tile flooring,

705
00:35:29,840 --> 00:35:34,400
creating a comfortable ambient temperature in the huge space.

706
00:35:34,401 --> 00:35:37,400
The steam that we use is calculated in these meters here

707
00:35:37,500 --> 00:35:39,140
that Con Edison comes to read.

708
00:35:39,240 --> 00:35:42,500
We're using far less steam than was originally projected

709
00:35:42,600 --> 00:35:45,440
due to the efficiency of our heating systems.

710
00:35:45,540 --> 00:35:49,140
While Con Ed boils water to provide steam heat,

711
00:35:49,240 --> 00:35:51,880
the goal of other companies, like the California Bottling

712
00:35:51,980 --> 00:35:55,420
Company, is to turn steam back into water.

713
00:35:55,520 --> 00:35:57,420
The facility processes and packages

714
00:35:57,520 --> 00:36:01,900
a variety of water products, including distilled water.

715
00:36:01,901 --> 00:36:05,140
Distillation involves boiling water and vaporizing it

716
00:36:05,240 --> 00:36:07,140
into steam.

717
00:36:07,240 --> 00:36:09,240
Boiling sterilizes the water.

718
00:36:09,340 --> 00:36:11,040
And when the steam lifts out of it,

719
00:36:11,140 --> 00:36:13,840
salts and other solids are left behind.

720
00:36:13,940 --> 00:36:15,440
Then the steam is cooled.

721
00:36:15,540 --> 00:36:17,740
And when it condenses back into liquid form,

722
00:36:17,840 --> 00:36:20,060
it's as close to pure H2O as possible.

723
00:36:22,660 --> 00:36:24,900
While distillation can produce clean water

724
00:36:25,000 --> 00:36:28,360
from a very contaminated source, California bottling

725
00:36:28,460 --> 00:36:31,900
begins with the municipal water supply.

726
00:36:31,901 --> 00:36:35,240
It arrives in the pipe above, fairly directly from the Sierras,

727
00:36:35,300 --> 00:36:36,240
as it turns out.

728
00:36:36,880 --> 00:36:39,640
But we treat it before processing through the distiller.

729
00:36:40,640 --> 00:36:42,540
First, we run it through some sand filters

730
00:36:42,541 --> 00:36:44,020
to take out particulate matter.

731
00:36:44,220 --> 00:36:46,940
We run it through carbon filters to take out chlorine

732
00:36:46,941 --> 00:36:47,880
that might be present.

733
00:36:48,540 --> 00:36:51,320
And then we run it through softeners to take out the hardness.

734
00:36:52,100 --> 00:36:54,680
All this is done to provide a very clean water

735
00:36:54,681 --> 00:36:56,920
before we even enter the distillation process.

736
00:36:57,560 --> 00:36:59,820
The facility has several stills.

737
00:36:59,821 --> 00:37:02,460
The largest output approximately 1,000 gallons

738
00:37:02,560 --> 00:37:05,360
of distilled water per hour.

739
00:37:05,460 --> 00:37:08,060
To eliminate any contaminants picked up in the system,

740
00:37:08,160 --> 00:37:11,360
the distilled water runs through submicron filters

741
00:37:11,460 --> 00:37:14,860
and then into a tank where ozone gas bubbles up through it

742
00:37:14,960 --> 00:37:18,580
to treat any remaining impurities.

743
00:37:18,680 --> 00:37:20,580
Now it's ready for bottling.

744
00:37:20,680 --> 00:37:23,280
This is about as pure as you can get for water.

745
00:37:23,380 --> 00:37:26,720
It's half a part or so per million of dissolved solids.

746
00:37:26,820 --> 00:37:28,520
The water in the gallon containers

747
00:37:28,521 --> 00:37:31,320
is an all-purpose product that's recommended for use

748
00:37:31,420 --> 00:37:32,760
in steam irons.

749
00:37:32,860 --> 00:37:35,320
But compared to regular water, distilled water

750
00:37:35,420 --> 00:37:37,320
is relatively tasteless.

751
00:37:37,420 --> 00:37:39,260
So when the California bottling company

752
00:37:39,360 --> 00:37:41,460
makes specialty-label drinking waters,

753
00:37:41,560 --> 00:37:45,040
these tanks are brought online to add back brand-specific

754
00:37:45,140 --> 00:37:47,080
electrolytes and minerals.

755
00:37:47,180 --> 00:37:49,480
These actually make the water healthier to drink

756
00:37:49,580 --> 00:37:52,380
and give it a more appealing taste.

757
00:37:52,480 --> 00:37:56,540
So steam becomes clean water, and it can run a power plant

758
00:37:56,541 --> 00:37:58,540
plant or heat a building.

759
00:37:58,640 --> 00:38:01,540
But can it inspire steamy art?

760
00:38:01,640 --> 00:38:02,640
Well, sort of.

761
00:38:06,340 --> 00:38:11,480
In 1852, French engineer Henri Giffard briefly flew a dirigible

762
00:38:11,580 --> 00:38:13,900
filled with hydrogen gas that was propelled

763
00:38:14,000 --> 00:38:17,800
by a three horsepower, 350 pound steam engine.

764
00:38:17,900 --> 00:38:21,200
His is considered the first flight in a powered aircraft.

765
00:38:26,540 --> 00:38:29,780
While this plotting, mechanized oddity

766
00:38:29,880 --> 00:38:32,780
may look like it just walked off the pages of a Jules Verne

767
00:38:32,880 --> 00:38:38,480
novel, it's actually part of a trend known as steampunk.

768
00:38:38,580 --> 00:38:41,780
The term has been around since the late 1980s to label

769
00:38:41,880 --> 00:38:45,300
contemporary drawings and objects designed with a pseudo-Victorian

770
00:38:45,400 --> 00:38:46,100
bent.

771
00:38:49,400 --> 00:38:52,500
In his garage in a quiet Central California neighborhood,

772
00:38:52,501 --> 00:38:55,340
neighborhood, I-Way Huang challenges himself

773
00:38:55,440 --> 00:39:00,440
to turn his steampunk art into a steam-powered reality.

774
00:39:00,540 --> 00:39:03,140
I've drawn steampunk kind of inspired art

775
00:39:03,240 --> 00:39:06,540
pretty much all my life, drawing old robots and stuff

776
00:39:06,640 --> 00:39:09,560
that uses old Victorian technology.

777
00:39:09,660 --> 00:39:12,360
And I put a lot of thought into how it might work,

778
00:39:12,460 --> 00:39:15,760
you know, and the technology behind it, so much so that I

779
00:39:15,860 --> 00:39:18,960
decided that it might actually work.

780
00:39:19,060 --> 00:39:21,260
I-Way, who makes a living working for a video game

781
00:39:21,261 --> 00:39:25,660
company, doesn't build his own parts.

782
00:39:25,760 --> 00:39:27,760
And the miniature steam engines he uses

783
00:39:27,860 --> 00:39:31,700
come from hobby stores. It's the unusual ways he cobbles

784
00:39:31,800 --> 00:39:33,800
everything together that grabs people's attention.

785
00:39:37,100 --> 00:39:38,820
This one actually won the best of show

786
00:39:38,920 --> 00:39:41,520
in RoboGames a couple of years back.

787
00:39:41,620 --> 00:39:43,820
It's in the shape of a trilobite, which

788
00:39:43,920 --> 00:39:49,720
is a prehistoric animal. Steam is very intriguing

789
00:39:49,721 --> 00:39:52,260
because you get to play with fire, you know,

790
00:39:52,360 --> 00:39:53,720
and you get to play with something

791
00:39:53,820 --> 00:39:56,460
that's a little bit dangerous, a little bit unusual.

792
00:39:56,560 --> 00:39:57,260
It's all visual.

793
00:39:57,360 --> 00:39:58,060
You can see it.

794
00:39:58,160 --> 00:39:59,660
You can smell it.

795
00:39:59,760 --> 00:40:02,060
So this is the steam rover.

796
00:40:02,160 --> 00:40:05,700
It's basically a steam engine powered,

797
00:40:05,800 --> 00:40:07,800
a six-wheel drive, four-wheel steering,

798
00:40:07,900 --> 00:40:10,280
little steam machine.

799
00:40:10,380 --> 00:40:13,140
It's one of the more powerful ones that I have,

800
00:40:13,240 --> 00:40:16,320
and it's just a blast to drive around.

801
00:40:16,420 --> 00:40:17,640
Most of E-Way's engines

802
00:40:17,641 --> 00:40:21,380
sound like tiny locomotives, but this one makes a distinctively

803
00:40:21,381 --> 00:40:22,380
different sound.

804
00:40:25,780 --> 00:40:30,180
I decided to do a turbine since I've done so many piston-driven

805
00:40:30,181 --> 00:40:31,880
steam machines.

806
00:40:31,881 --> 00:40:36,400
So it was a challenge finding a turbine that actually works

807
00:40:36,401 --> 00:40:37,300
well.

808
00:40:37,301 --> 00:40:40,800
It uses up a lot of steam very, very fast and has super high

809
00:40:40,801 --> 00:40:43,100
RPM but not a whole lot of torque.

810
00:40:43,101 --> 00:40:45,640
So a lot of gearing down is required.

811
00:40:45,641 --> 00:40:47,980
And the forward reverse is done by a clutch

812
00:40:48,080 --> 00:40:50,880
because the turbine only turns one way.

813
00:40:50,980 --> 00:40:52,440
This is the steam centipede.

814
00:40:52,540 --> 00:40:55,580
It's one of my most complex machines that I've built.

815
00:40:55,680 --> 00:40:57,380
It has 32 legs.

816
00:40:57,480 --> 00:41:00,520
In old science fiction movies, there's

817
00:41:00,620 --> 00:41:02,820
a lot of steam walking machines.

818
00:41:02,920 --> 00:41:06,360
But there's a reason why it's really hard to pull off.

819
00:41:06,460 --> 00:41:08,500
It's just not as efficient as wheels.

820
00:41:11,100 --> 00:41:13,760
Iwei has managed to get even his most complex designs

821
00:41:13,761 --> 00:41:16,400
up and running on a small scale.

822
00:41:16,500 --> 00:41:18,460
But what about a full-sized version?

823
00:41:18,560 --> 00:41:22,800
I would love to see one of these huge steam machines,

824
00:41:22,900 --> 00:41:25,200
something like this, that can carry people around.

825
00:41:25,300 --> 00:41:29,300
But this already scares me that the boilers could fail

826
00:41:29,400 --> 00:41:32,420
on a big scale. That's not something

827
00:41:32,520 --> 00:41:34,920
I want to think about.

828
00:41:35,020 --> 00:41:37,660
But in nearby Oakland, a group of artisans

829
00:41:37,760 --> 00:41:40,920
operating under the Kinetic Steamworks banner

830
00:41:41,020 --> 00:41:43,220
take on the challenge of dealing with fire and water

831
00:41:43,221 --> 00:41:49,460
on a much bigger scale, with sometimes startling results.

832
00:41:49,560 --> 00:41:52,160
Here at Kinetic Steamworks, we have a complete reverence

833
00:41:52,260 --> 00:41:55,000
for the vintage equipment and the stylings of the way things

834
00:41:55,100 --> 00:41:56,100
used to be done.

835
00:41:56,200 --> 00:41:58,600
But we really like to take the vintage equipment

836
00:41:58,700 --> 00:42:03,900
and then use it for contemporary art pieces.

837
00:42:04,000 --> 00:42:07,480
Like their 1920 traction engine named Hortense

838
00:42:07,580 --> 00:42:09,740
that provided the steam power for a belt drive that

839
00:42:09,840 --> 00:42:11,240
turned a carousel.

840
00:42:11,241 --> 00:42:13,240
There were actually steam carousels back in the day.

841
00:42:13,340 --> 00:42:16,180
This was a little bit more on an industrial scale,

842
00:42:16,280 --> 00:42:20,880
and it really was a massive belt movement and gear motion,

843
00:42:20,980 --> 00:42:22,780
reciprocating motion.

844
00:42:22,880 --> 00:42:24,980
That was much of a pleasure to ride the carousel

845
00:42:25,080 --> 00:42:27,280
as it was to watch the entire operation.

846
00:42:30,800 --> 00:42:34,300
At the Kinetic Steamworks 6,000-square-foot shop,

847
00:42:34,400 --> 00:42:37,200
the group fires up a small boiler to supply steam

848
00:42:37,300 --> 00:42:39,800
to some of their smaller projects.

849
00:42:39,801 --> 00:42:42,300
The boiler is called the blister by some,

850
00:42:42,301 --> 00:42:45,300
and little baby steamy pants by some others.

851
00:42:45,301 --> 00:42:47,300
We have a firebox,

852
00:42:47,301 --> 00:42:51,300
and right now we're burning liquid fuel and wood.

853
00:42:51,301 --> 00:42:53,300
Here comes some steam.

854
00:42:57,300 --> 00:43:00,800
This is a mock-up of what might have been used in a factory

855
00:43:00,801 --> 00:43:03,800
at the turn of the century, the last century,

856
00:43:03,801 --> 00:43:05,300
not the closest one,

857
00:43:05,301 --> 00:43:08,300
where you've got steam coming to a small engine like this,

858
00:43:08,301 --> 00:43:11,300
and there's a PTO, a power takeoff here,

859
00:43:11,301 --> 00:43:15,300
that the sewing machine operator can tension this belt

860
00:43:15,301 --> 00:43:16,300
or detension it.

861
00:43:16,301 --> 00:43:18,300
It just basically takes steam power,

862
00:43:18,301 --> 00:43:20,300
turns it into centrifugal force,

863
00:43:20,301 --> 00:43:23,300
and makes the flywheel on the sewing machine work.

864
00:43:23,301 --> 00:43:27,300
Not everything we do here is practical at Kinetic Steamworks.

865
00:43:27,301 --> 00:43:31,300
However, art can be a lot of fun.

866
00:43:31,301 --> 00:43:33,300
This is a sculpture that I put together

867
00:43:33,301 --> 00:43:35,300
out of old musical instruments.

868
00:43:35,301 --> 00:43:37,300
One of the things that we as an organization did

869
00:43:37,301 --> 00:43:40,900
did was restore a steamboat, a paddle wheel, stern wheel

870
00:43:41,000 --> 00:43:44,300
steamboat, and it was a beautiful experience.

871
00:43:44,400 --> 00:43:47,440
The artist that we collaborated with, Swoon,

872
00:43:47,540 --> 00:43:49,440
she decorated our vessel.

873
00:43:49,540 --> 00:43:52,240
And despite the fact that it didn't necessarily

874
00:43:52,340 --> 00:43:57,360
look like a steamboat of the 1800s, it ran like one.

875
00:43:57,460 --> 00:44:00,560
The steamboat now sits quietly in the back of the shop

876
00:44:00,660 --> 00:44:04,600
while the Steamworks gang is dreaming up their next project.

877
00:44:04,601 --> 00:44:07,600
In the meantime, let's just blow off a little steam.

